## Summary
Implement `packages/ai-eval/src/chatflow-client.ts` — the single place we hit the chatflow API. Signature per the plan:

```ts
export async function callChatflow(opts: {
  apiHost: string;
  apiToken: string;
  chatflowId: string;
  question: string;
  chatId: string;
  trackingMetadata: Record<string, unknown>;
  signal?: AbortSignal;
}): Promise<{ text: string; raw: unknown }>;
```

POSTs to `{apiHost}/api/v1/prediction/{chatflowId}` with `Authorization: Bearer {apiToken}`. Body includes `question`, `streaming: false`, `chatId`, `trackingMetadata`. Throws `ChatflowHTTPError` on non-2xx with `status` + `bodyExcerpt` (first 500 chars, with the bearer token regex-redacted). Propagates `AbortSignal`.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] `apiHost` trailing slash normalized
- [ ] `ChatflowHTTPError` class with `status`, `bodyExcerpt`, `cause` — `bodyExcerpt` runs the input through a regex that strips any `Bearer xxx` and any literal occurrence of the apiToken value
- [ ] Network errors map to `ChatflowNetworkError` with redacted message
- [ ] `signal` propagation tested (calling abort cancels in-flight fetch)
- [ ] `text` extraction prefers `data.text`, then `data.response`, then `JSON.stringify(data)` fallback
- [ ] Unit tests: happy path, 4xx, 5xx, abort, malformed JSON, **token-never-in-error-message regression** (assert error.message and error.bodyExcerpt do not contain the input token even when the upstream echoes it back)
- [ ] Module marked server-only — accidental client import trips the build

## Summary
Implement `packages/ai-eval/src/queue.ts` — one rolling-concurrency engine reused by both apps' run processors. Replaces the duplicated logic in the source repo's `useQuestions.js` and `useCSVChatflow.js`.

Shape:
```ts
export async function runRollingQueue<TInput, TResult>(opts: {
  items: TInput[];
  concurrency: number;
  signal?: AbortSignal;
  shouldCancel?: () => Promise<boolean> | boolean; // checked between rows
  process: (item: TInput, index: number, signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<TResult>;
  onComplete?: (item: TInput, index: number, result: TResult | { error: unknown }) => Promise<void>;
}): Promise<{ completed: number; errored: number; cancelled: boolean }>;
```

Guarantees:
- Never more than `concurrency` in-flight at once
- Each row's `process` receives a per-row AbortSignal that fires when either the outer signal aborts or `shouldCancel()` returns true
- `onComplete` runs exactly once per item, even on error
- Errors in one item do not abort the others (caller decides via `onComplete`)

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] In-flight cap never exceeded under any input size (verified by recording max concurrency in test)
- [ ] Abort signal propagates to in-flight items and prevents new ones from starting
- [ ] `shouldCancel()` checked at least once per row gap; returning true stops new dispatches
- [ ] `onComplete` called for every item that started, including ones that errored
- [ ] Returns accurate `completed` / `errored` / `cancelled` counts
- [ ] Unit tests cover: cap enforcement, abort, mid-run cancel, all-errors case, empty-input edge case, concurrency=1 (sequential) edge case
